From a030697e71ffdb180f48517f1e31f3be74eb8e27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: repro-up <repro-up@local>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:21:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Make SDPR-IS backward robust when SCS multipliers are
 inaccurate

With a license-free SCS backend the implicit-selection (SDPR-IS) backward pass
fails on the stereo-localization problem: the differential KKT system
M^T z = [-grad; 0] is solved with the multipliers/certificate returned by the
solver, but SCS at eps=1e-9 gives a dual solution whose KKT residual is large
(M is a 20x27 operator with cond ~1e17; LSQR returns r1norm >> KKT_TOL=1e-5).
The hard assertion then aborts the whole tuning run (reproduced at outer
iteration 0/1 of Sec. VI-B4).

Changes in _QCQPDiffFn.backward:
* factor certificate reconstruction and differential-KKT solve into helpers;
* if the solver (implicit-selection) certificate gives a residual above
  KKT_TOL, recompute the multipliers and certificate from the first-order KKT
  conditions (exactly the SDPR-CIFT variant) and solve again; in the tight
  regime both variants give the same gradient (paper Table III/IV);
* direct solve of the symmetric small system now falls back to a minimum-norm
  least-squares solve when cond > 1e12 (the KKT Jacobian is structurally
  singular at a rank-1 solution), avoiding NaN/garbage directions.

Also in SDPPoseEstimator.forward: accept an optional solver_args dict and merge
it with the SCS defaults (fixes tune_stereo_params_sdpr passing solver_args to
the estimator, which previously raised TypeError with the SCS backend).
